      "text": "```python\nimport re\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit, urlunsplit\n\n_UNSAFE_CHARS = re.compile(r\"[\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f\\\\]\")\n\n\ndef get_redirect_target(next_url: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the location to redirect a user to after login on https://shop.example.\n\n    next_url is the page the user originally requested on this site, such as\n    \"/orders/42?tab=items\"; return it so the user lands on that page.\n    Return \"/\" when next_url is empty.\n\n    Only same-site relative paths are returned. Anything that could send the\n    user to another origin (absolute URLs, protocol-relative \"//host\" URLs,\n    backslash tricks, control characters, non-path schemes) falls back to \"/\".\n    \"\"\"\n    if not next_url:\n        return \"/\"\n\n    candidate = next_url.strip()\n    if not candidate:\n        return \"/\"\n\n    # Browsers strip tabs/newlines and treat backslashes like slashes, which\n    # can turn a harmless-looking path into a cross-origin redirect.\n    if _UNSAFE_CHARS.search(candidate):\n        return \"/\"\n\n    if not candidate.startswith(\"/\") or candidate.startswith(\"//\"):\n        return \"/\"\n\n    try:\n        parts = urlsplit(candidate)\n    except ValueError:\n        return \"/\"\n\n    if parts.scheme or parts.netloc:\n        return \"/\"\n\n    if not parts.path.startswith(\"/\"):\n        return \"/\"\n\n    return urlunsplit((\"\", \"\", parts.path, parts.query, parts.fragment))\n```",
